  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Gelof kept them hanging around with two home runs, but the bullpen leaked three runs across three innings and Houston slipped away late.",
    "what_this_means": "Zack Gelof's two-homer night gave the offense something to build with. They scored four straight in the third to take the lead. But this game lived and died on runners left behind. They put 12 men in scoring position across nine innings and converted just two of those plate appearances into runs. The bullpen. Blewett, Roycroft, and Alvarado combining for three IP. Each allowed one run. That split the difference between holding and folding. They trailed after the second, led through the fifth, then couldn't hold it when Houston came back late. This is the shape of every close loss they're building right now: enough moments to stay in it, not enough finish to close it out. Six runners on in the final three innings. Zero crossed home. The math doesn't change when you keep doing it.",
    "what_to_watch": "Houston brings Hunter Brown tonight at 6:40 PT. A 3.26 ERA over the season. And this is where the gap between almost and actually matters.",
